NAME
    rm - delete a file or directory

SYNOPSIS
    rm <path>
    rm -r <path>

SUSPENDED
    This command currently refuses to do anything. A published build puts
    its own binaries in '/lib/EmuSen', inside this root - see 'man hier' -
    and nothing in this shell tells the install apart from your data, so
    'rm -r /lib' would delete the running application. Parked until the
    tree distinguishes the two. Everything below describes what it does
    when re-enabled.

DESCRIPTION
    Deletes a real file. A directory requires -r (matching real rm's own
    refusal to remove a directory without it); with -r, the directory and
    everything inside it is deleted, no further confirmation (no -i prompt
    anywhere in this shell, by design, same as 'mv'). Must resolve inside
    the project's own directory tree - see 'cd' - and refuses to remove that
    root directory itself even with -r, so 'rm -r .' from the project root
    (or 'rm -r' on the root's own path) can't wipe out the whole project.
    Like 'cd', rm only ever takes one path, so a directory name with spaces
    doesn't need quoting ('rm -r My Folder' works) - everything after the
    optional -r is treated as the literal path.

EXAMPLES
    rm scratch.txt
    rm -r home/Logs/OldRun
    rm -r My Folder
